Ladybird fits lines against floats using their final aligned height. It
retries line breaking when alignment grows a line into another float
band, restoring tentative float and margin state before retrying. The
height used for fitting never decreases during a retry, even if wrapping
removes the tall content. Final placement wraps beside the floats or
clears them when an unbreakable run doesn’t fit.

Blink’s InlineLayoutAlgorithm::Layout() uses the final line height from
ComputeTotalBlockSize() to check the available layout opportunity. If
the line is too tall, it tries another opportunity. Shape exclusions can
also make it retry line breaking with a different width. Gecko’s
nsBlockFrame::PlaceLine() checks the final aligned line. It calls
VerticalAlignLine(), then GetFloatAvailableSpaceForBSize(), and requests
RedoMoreFloats if the available width shrinks.

WebKit’s LineBuilder::adjustedLineRectWithCandidateInlineContent()
checks candidate text line-height and atomic margin-box height before
fitting. Its InlineFormattingContext::lineLayout() builds the aligned
line box afterward. The inspected path doesn’t show a general refit with
that final height. Ladybird also fits vertical-align shifts using the
final extent, matching Blink and Gecko’s behavior.

Chrome 157.0.8103.78 wraps the nested 50px line-height example beside a
float starting at y=30. It moves an unbreakable 144px word below the
float. Ladybird matches both results, and the measured tall-inline-block
and vertical-align cases.
